 "what_this_is": "The specification's second proof track, answered at MODEL level and NOT measured. Section 38 asks whether one planner arbitrating a shared budget across two tensor classes beats two independent policies. Through 0.2.0 the answer under this repository's own cost model was provably no: the model was linear in the resident share, total saving was a fractional knapsack, greedy-on-density was optimal, and the global arm's one distinguishing action -- telling the weight stream to get out of the way -- was priced at exactly zero. 0.2.1 replaced that model with one fitted to the paired hardware arms in results/, and fixed two independent bugs that made a Stream action unreachable under a cyclic decode graph (emit_stream_hints required a tensor with NO reuse, and the CUDA executor skipped a Stream action under capture). Under the new model the global arm wins on all three golden traces. EVERY NUMBER HERE IS A COST-MODEL OUTPUT: a falsifiable prediction about hardware, not evidence about a speedup. Only eval/decide.py --real and tools/tt-frontier are evidence about a speedup.",
 "basis": "model",
 "generated_utc": "2026-09-10T06:58:27+00:00",
 "how_to_reproduce": {
  "residency": "tensortransit compare tests/golden/<trace>.json --json",
  "linear_control": "tensortransit compare tests/golden/<trace>.json --cost-model linear --json",
  "mechanism_control": "tensortransit compare tests/golden/<trace>.json --stream-relief 0 --json"
 },
 "reading": "Under `linear` the global arm cannot beat the best single-role arm -- that is the negative result 0.2.0 published, preserved here as the control. Under `residency` it does. Under `residency` with stream_relief 0 the advantage disappears again, which identifies the mechanism as the Stream action rather than as a number that came out higher. Two tests assert exactly this, and a third asserts the other half: on a workload whose weight traffic IS holdable no Stream action is emitted, and the global arm's advantage there is role_floor arbitration instead.",
